Finnish law says nothing about hitchhiking (''liftaus''), so it is legal wherever a pedestrian may lawfully be. [https://www.finlex.fi/fi/laki/ajantasa/2018/20180729 Section 58 of the ''tieliikennelaki'' (729/2018)] allows only motor-vehicle traffic on a ''moottoritie'' or ''moottoriliikennetie'', which keeps you off the carriageway, the shoulder and the slip road. Section 37 forbids stopping on those roads anywhere except a signposted parking place, ''palvelualue'' or ''levähdysalue'' — and, usefully, at a signposted bus stop, for the purpose of picking up or setting down a passenger. Service areas and rest areas are therefore both where you ask drivers and the only places they may lawfully stop for you. On every other road hitchhiking is unregulated: stand on the verge, facing the traffic.
